<h2>India's largest groundnut kernel market, by a wide margin</h2>
<p>Of every country we ship HPS groundnut kernels to, Indonesia takes the most — around 36% of India's total 2024 export value on this product, roughly USD 295 million. That scale reflects Indonesia's food-processing industry, which runs confectionery, snack and peanut-product manufacturing at a volume few other markets match, and it's consistent with why groundnut kernels are India's single largest agri-export line overall (see the <a href="/products/oilseeds/groundnut-kernels/">groundnut kernels product page</a> for the full cited country breakdown).</p>

<h2>Two different Indonesian buyers, two different specs</h2>
<p>Confectionery and snack manufacturers tend to want a consistent, larger kernel count — 38/42 or 40/44 — because size and appearance matter for the finished product. Oil-extraction buyers care much less about kernel size and much more about oil content and consistency of supply at volume. If you're buying on behalf of a processor, tell us which of these two profiles you fit; it changes what grade makes sense and, often, the price point.</p>

<h2>Halal and BPOM: plan for them, don't scramble at shipment time</h2>
<p>Indonesia runs one of the world's more formalised halal certification systems (administered through BPJPH), and most food products entering the domestic market also need BPOM registration. Neither is unusual or difficult if it's planned for — the mistake we see buyers make is treating these as something to sort out once the goods are already packed. Raise your certifying-body and BPOM-registration requirements when you first enquire and we'll build the documentation timeline around it.</p>

<h2>Logistics and terms</h2>
<p>Transit runs 10–16 days from Indian ports to Jakarta's Tanjung Priok, Indonesia's main container gateway. USD invoicing is standard, with both LC and TT used — Indonesia's larger food manufacturers tend to be experienced, well-documented counterparties.</p>

